
  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153muserId[39m is something the [3muser supplies[0m, something the [3mclient generates[0m, or something that shouldn't
  be a parameter at all because [38;5;153musername[39m already identifies who logged in.

  - [1mAdd a userId field to the form[0m — buys you a real caller-supplied value, costs a UI change ([38;5;153mindex.html[39m +
    [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m), and commits you to asking users for an ID they may not know. Hard to walk back once it's shipped in
    the UI.
  - [1mClient-generated tracking ID[0m — buys you a per-attempt correlation ID for logs without touching the form; costs
    nothing in UI, but it identifies the [3msession[0m, not the person, so it won't answer "who logged in" on its own.
  - [1mDon't add the parameter; log [38;5;153musername[0m — buys you the tracking you described with zero interface change, costs
    nothing, and rules nothing out. Cheapest to reverse.
  - [1mReturn userId from login instead[0m — the server tells you who; the parameter direction is backwards from the request.
    Changes the return shape, not the signature.

  [1mMy recommendation: the third or fourth.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m already receives [38;5;153musername[39m and already logs it — if the goal is
  literally "track who logged in," a parameter the caller has to invent is a weaker signal than the identity the user
  just typed. Adding a required third parameter also silently breaks the existing call at [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m unless I update it
  in the same change. But if [38;5;153muserId[39m is a real ID from a system you haven't shown me, that changes the answer.
